## You will end with a working /test command

You repeat yourself in chat: same test instructions, same git steps, every day. A custom command saves that prompt under a slash name. You will build `/test`, then see the pattern in a real example. Takes about 5 minutes.

## Meet /ship in 30 seconds

`/ship` is our running example. It turns messy git output into clean commits.

You type:

```sh
/ship
/ship push
```

It does:

- Reads `git status --short`, `git diff --stat`, `git log --oneline -10`
- Groups changed files by concern (feature, fix, docs)
- Commits each group with a short lowercase message
- Only runs `git push` when you add `push`

It ends with proof:

```sh
a1b2c3d locations (3 files)
Tree: clean
Pushed: no
```

No slash file, no shortcut. With the file, one line replaces five manual steps. Next you will see the file behind it.

## Prerequisites

- OpenCode installed and opening in TUI mode
- A project folder (any repo works)
- 2 minutes after each edit to quit and restart OpenCode (config loads at startup, no hot-reload)

No agent setup needed. If you omit the `agent` field, the command runs in your current agent. That is what you want here.

## Step 1: pick global vs. project scope

Two folders, same format ([Commands](https://opencode.ai/docs/commands/) + [Config locations](https://opencode.ai/docs/config/#locations)):

- **Global:** available in every project, personal workflows
- **Project:** `.opencode/commands/` in your repo root, checked into git, shared with your team

Global path per OS ([Troubleshooting](https://opencode.ai/docs/troubleshooting/) uses the same pattern for config and plugins, `commands/` follows it):

| OS              | Global commands folder                                              |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| macOS / Linux   | `~/.config/opencode/commands/` (`~` = `/Users/you` or `/home/you`)  |
| Windows native  | `%USERPROFILE%\.config\opencode\commands\` (`C:\Users\You\.config\opencode\commands\`) |
| Windows via WSL | `~/.config/opencode/commands/` **inside WSL** ([run OpenCode in WSL](https://opencode.ai/docs/windows-wsl/)) |

Filename becomes command name. `ship.md` gives `/ship`. `test.md` gives `/test`.

Use project scope below so the example stays inside your repo.

## Step 2: dissect the file behind /ship

`/ship` is not built-in. It is a plain file. This is the beginner version (full version adds an agent, skipped here):

```md
---
description: Bundle working tree into short commits (add "push" to also push)
---

Inspect the working tree with `git status --short`, `git diff --stat`, and
`git log --oneline -10`. Bundle changes by concern and commit each bundle with
a short lowercase message (≤50 chars) matching the repo's existing style.

Arguments: $ARGUMENTS. If arguments contain the word `push`, `git push`
afterwards (never force). Otherwise stop after committing.

End with `git status --short` proof.
```

Three pieces ([Commands](https://opencode.ai/docs/commands/#create-command-files)):

1. **Filename** `ship.md` — stored in your global commands folder (table above), this is what makes `/ship` exist
2. **Frontmatter `description`** — one sentence shown in TUI autocomplete
3. **Body = template** — the prompt OpenCode runs. `$ARGUMENTS` is whatever you type after `/ship`, so `/ship push` passes `push` into the template and triggers the push branch above.

Advanced versions add `agent:` and `subtask:` to run as a background subagent. Skip both for your first commands.

## Step 3: create your /test command

Create `.opencode/commands/test.md`:

```md
---
description: Run tests with coverage
---

Run the full test suite with coverage report and show any failures.
Focus on the failing tests and suggest fixes.
```

That is a complete command. Description plus plain-English prompt, nothing else.

## Step 4: accept input with $ARGUMENTS

Static prompts get old fast. `$ARGUMENTS` makes one file reusable.

Create `.opencode/commands/component.md`:

```md
---
description: Create a new component
---

Create a new React component named $ARGUMENTS with TypeScript support.
Include proper typing and basic structure.
```

Run `/component Button` and `$ARGUMENTS` becomes `Button`.

Same idea, for later ([Arguments](https://opencode.ai/docs/commands/#arguments), [Shell output](https://opencode.ai/docs/commands/#shell-output), [File references](https://opencode.ai/docs/commands/#file-references)):

- `$1`, `$2`, `$3` pull positional words (`$1` is the first word after the command)
- `` !`command` `` pastes shell output into the prompt, e.g. `` !`git log --oneline -10` ``
- `@path/to/file` includes a file, e.g. `Review @src/components/Button.tsx`

Start with `$ARGUMENTS`. Add the rest only when you hit its limit.

## Step 5: verify it works

1. Quit and restart OpenCode
2. Type `/` in the TUI — `/test` and `/component` should appear with their descriptions
3. Run `/test` — output should follow your template wording
4. Run `/component Button` — result should mention `Button` by name

If the names do not appear, the files are in the wrong folder or misnamed. Filenames must be lowercase letters, numbers, and hyphens, ending in `.md`.

## Troubleshooting

- **Command missing after save?** You edited but did not restart. OpenCode reads commands once at startup.
- **Template ignored?** Only text below the second `---` runs as prompt ([the body becomes the template](https://opencode.ai/docs/commands/#create-command-files)). Frontmatter never runs.
- **Wrong scope?** Check the right folder for your OS:
  - macOS / Linux: `ls ~/.config/opencode/commands/` for global, `ls .opencode/commands/` for project
  - Windows native (PowerShell): `dir "$env:USERPROFILE\.config\opencode\commands\"`
  - Windows via WSL: `ls ~/.config/opencode/commands/` inside WSL
- **Want JSON instead?** Same fields work inline in `opencode.json` under `"command": { "test": { "template": "...", "description": "..." } }` ([JSON config](https://opencode.ai/docs/commands/#json)). Markdown files are easier to edit, so prefer them ([Markdown files](https://opencode.ai/docs/commands/#markdown)).
